# SmartHire

**Platform:** HackTheBox
**OS:** Linux
**Tags:** MLflow, Default Credentials, Pickle Deserialization, cloudpickle, RCE, .pth Hijack
**Date:** 2026-08-21

An AI hiring platform with a Flask web app backed by an MLflow tracking server on a subdomain. MLflow was running on its documented default credentials giving full write access to the artifact store. The trained model loads a custom pickle on every prediction, so overwriting it with a malicious one and triggering a prediction drops a shell as `svcweb`. Root comes from a `sudo NOPASSWD` script that calls `site.addsitedir()` on a directory the service user can write to. That function processes `.pth` files as code at import time, before `main()` even runs.

